dhcpc (4) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * Add dhcpc -a to supervise one client per Ethernet interface,
    including interfaces attached after startup. The supervisor runs in
    the foreground and restarts stopped clients.
  * Keep valid leases across interface removal and link changes.
    Revalidate the lease when the link or the same adapter returns,
    request a new lease if the hardware address changes, and recover
    existing leases after a client restart.
  * Coordinate the default route and DNS settings across interfaces,
    preferring confirmed leases and then the lowest-numbered eligible
    interface. If no interface is eligible yet, preserve the current
    route and DNS settings while confirming an address left without a
    lease record after a soft reboot or upgrade.
  * Maintain interface-scoped subnet routes and restore missing routes
    when applying a lease. Avoid resetting unchanged addresses and routes
    so ARP entries survive renewals.
  * Record leases and managed routes for recovery after client restarts,
    and retry failed address configuration and cleanup.
  * Renew leases by unicast to the server through the leased interface;
    broadcast when rebinding. Validate reply headers, required fields
    and nonzero UDP checksums, and support overloaded and repeated
    options.
  * Log lease activity and link changes to /var/log/dhcpc.log.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 04:31:15 +0000

dhcpc (3) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * Build-Depend on pietoolchain-host and pietoolchain-sdk.
  * dhcpc writes /var/run/resolv.conf, and /etc/resolv.conf is a link to
    it, so /etc need not be writable. The link replaces a resolv.conf that
    dhcpc wrote. A resolv.conf written by hand stays, and dhcpc's name
    servers are not used then. An upgrade restarts the running dhcpc
    processes, which would otherwise replace the link.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 20:12:33 +0000

dhcpc (2) blueberry-pie-os; urgency=medium

  * A clock step no longer ends a lease early. The lease is timed from
    when the request was sent, by time since the system started.
  * A lease that can't be renewed ends on time. A T1 at or past the
    lease's end is taken as half the lease.
  * A lease the leasing server refuses ends at once. Before T2, a
    refusal from any other server is ignored. A refused first request
    starts over at once. A second refusal ends the try.

 -- Maxwell <maxwell@blueberrypieos.com>  Wed, 11 Nov 2026 13:48:21 +0000

